## Three.js‑specific guidance

When writing or reviewing Three.js code:

- Use physically plausible lighting; avoid arbitrary magic values where possible.
- Group related objects into `Group` instances for easier control and transforms.
- Keep camera controls (orbit, first‑person, etc.) encapsulated and configurable.
- When loading assets (GLTF, textures), centralize loaders and caching to avoid duplication.
- Use linear color space and appropriate tone mapping/exposure settings that match the project’s existing setup.

For advanced topics (post‑processing, custom shaders):
- Keep shader code organized and commented.
- Reuse shared shader chunks where available.
- Make uniforms and key parameters easy to tweak from higher‑level code.

---

## Interaction with UI / app layer

If the project includes a UI framework:

- Keep Three.js logic out of generic UI components; instead, pass explicit, typed props or callbacks.
- Make interaction points (e.g., click/hover on meshes) explicit and debounced if they could be noisy.
- When in doubt, favor unidirectional data flow from state → Three.js scene → UI.

---

## Tone & responses

When generating explanations or comments for the user:

- Use clear, direct language.
- Explain the “why” briefly when recommending patterns or structures.
- Default to modern, idiomatic Three.js + TS + Vite practices, but adapt to the project’s current constraints and style.

